MMQ: per-channel weight pointers, so a MoE launch reads each expert where it lies

Strata's prompt path multiplies a group of up to 16 experts in one mul_mat_q launch (a channel per expert).  The
kernel finds channel c's matrix at x + c * stride_channel_x blocks, so Strata copied every expert out of its cache
slot (or staging-ring slot) into one group buffer first: 1.5-4 MB an expert, about 9% of the GPU time of a 2,048-token
prompt chunk on two Radeon PRO V620 ("dequant" in STRATA_PREFILL_TIMING).

mmq_args gets `x_each` ({const char * p[MMQ_X_EACH_MAX]; int n}).  With n > 0 the kernel takes channel c's matrix
from p[c] and reads neither `x` nor stride_channel_x; n == 0 (what llama.cpp's own callers leave it at, and Strata
without STRATA_PF_DIRECT=1) is the stock addressing.  The same blocks reach the same tile loaders: the output is bit
for bit the same (tests/hip/prefill_expert_bench.cpp checks every expert weight type with the experts apart and
FP16 NaNs between them).

Strata uses it for gate/up only (K 2560).  mul_mat_q reads K in steps of MMQ_ITER_K = 256 values, so 128 values past
each row of a 640-column down matrix, and past the matrix for its last row; in the group buffer a zeroed tail makes
that harmless, in a cache slot the next bytes are whatever was there.

Base: llama.cpp 3cf03257f219afbe7334045ff7c6a06ac68c627d with 0001 and 0002 applied (mmq.cuh: it applies after 0002).
Apply:  patch -p1 -d third_party/llama.cpp < third_party/llama.cpp-patches/0003-mmq-per-channel-weight-pointers.patch
Remove: the same with -R.  Without it Strata builds as before and STRATA_PF_DIRECT=1 covers Q4_0 / Q4_1 only
(src/prefill/moe_mmq.cu tests MMQ_X_EACH_MAX).

